     <p>Why can $L^\star$ jump at $\kappa=1$ although $f$ changes continuously at every input? $L^\star$ is a supremum over all inputs, including distant ones. For $\kappa\lt1$ the steep both-active region can be a thin wedge, or lie far away, and still determine that supremum; at $\kappa=1$ its two boundaries coincide, the region loses its interior and its Jacobian no longer counts. Sampling in one fixed square can miss the region long before it disappears.</p>
 
