     <p><strong>What to try.</strong> (1) Raise $\sigma_g$: the certified ellipse (green) shrinks away from the grey $\sigma_g=0$ certificate and the inner set grows; at $n_g=40$ nothing is certified beyond $\sigma_g=0.055$, a finer grid recovers it. (2) Switch to the composite constant: about four times the largest cell bound on the box and, at the default settings, about 6.5 to 60 times the cell bounds on the certified set; nothing is certified at any grid size offered, which is why fine grids and local constants are used in practice. (3) Set $\vartheta\lt1$: the certificate grows but is no longer backed by a bound, and the trajectories usually still behave, which is exactly why such shortcuts are tempting. (4) Compare with the shaded capture region, simulated for one sampled $g$ (empirical, not a certificate): the certificate is limited by the shape of $V$, not only by uncertainty.</p>
 
